---
title: Assertions and CEL invariants
intent: Check response values, operators and cross-field expressions against a local fixture.
group: core
features:
- cel
command: curlew run testapi/collections/10-assertions.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
order: 50
runnable: true
---

CEL assertions use `response`; conditional requests use `previous`, `vars` and `env`. The complete fixture below exercises known response shapes. Validation checks expression structure before execution; runtime field types still matter.

## Prerequisites

Use the complete repository checkout and start Mudflat as described in the [cookbook setup](https://github.com/weiqigod/curlew/blob/main/site/README.md). Run these commands from the repository root with `curlew` on PATH.

```bash
export MUDFLAT_URL="${MUDFLAT_URL:-http://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export MUDFLAT_WS_URL="${MUDFLAT_WS_URL:-ws://127.0.0.1:18080}"
export RUN_ID="cookbook-$(date +%s)-$$"
```

## Run it

```bash
curlew run testapi/collections/10-assertions.yaml --var mud="$MUDFLAT_URL" --var ws="$MUDFLAT_WS_URL" --var run="$RUN_ID"
```

A successful execution exits 0. Read assertion outcomes as well as the exit code.
No example output or timing numbers are fabricated here.

## Complete collection

<!-- cookbook-source: testapi/collections/10-assertions.yaml -->
```yaml
# Every assertion operator, against response shapes chosen to break naive
# implementations.
#
# The bignum and dupkeys requests are the ones worth reading: both produce
# valid JSON that a careless parser silently changes.
name: Assertions against difficult bodies
description: |
  Exercises the 13 body operators, the 3 header operators, status, timing and
  CEL, against a server that emits genuinely awkward documents.

variables:
  expected_variant: "vendor"

requests:
  - name: status any-of form
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/status/418"
    assertions:
      status: [200, 418, 503]
      body:
        $.status: { equals: 418 }
        $.status_text: { equals: "I'm a teapot" }

  - name: bodiless 204 carries nothing
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/status/204"
    assertions:
      status: 204

  - name: integers beyond float64 survive intact
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/bignum"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        # 9007199254740993 is not representable as a float64: a round trip
        # through one yields ...92. An equals that passes against the wrong
        # number is worse than one that fails.
        $.beyond_float64: { equals: 9007199254740993 }
        $.negative: { less_than: 0 }
        $.very_large: { greater_than: 1000000 }
      cel:
        - "response.body.beyond_float64 == 9007199254740993"

  - name: duplicate keys resolve consistently
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/dupkeys"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.a: { exists: true }
        $.b: { type: string }

  - name: deep nesting is addressable
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/deep"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a.a: { exists: true }

  - name: top-level array
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/toplevel-array"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $: { type: array, length: 4 }
        $[0]: { equals: 1 }
        $[3].nested: { equals: true }

  - name: top-level null
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/toplevel-null"
    assertions:
      status: 200

  - name: empty object
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/empty"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $: { length: 0 }

  - name: unicode escapes stay escaped on the wire
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/unicode-escapes"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.latin: { type: string }
        $.surrogate_pair: { exists: true }

  - name: bare NaN is not valid JSON
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/nan"
    assertions:
      # No body assertions: the point of this endpoint is that the body cannot
      # be parsed. Asserting only the status proves the response still arrives.
      status: 200

  - name: header operators
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/content-type/vendor"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "application/vnd.api+json" }
        Content-Length: { matches: "^[0-9]+$" }
        # exists: false asserts ABSENCE. It used to behave identically to
        # exists: true, so "this response must not carry X" was unexpressible.
        X-Definitely-Not-Sent: { exists: false }
        Set-Cookie: { exists: false }
      body:
        # A body parser keyed on an exact application/json match would skip a
        # vendor type and report path-not-found instead of failing loudly.
        # The expected value comes from a collection variable: assertion values
        # interpolate, which they did not until the first dogfood run found it.
        $.variant: { equals: "{{expected_variant}}" }

  - name: charset-qualified json is still json
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/content-type/json-charset"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "application/json; charset=utf-8" }
      body:
        $.ok: { equals: true }

  - name: numeric comparison operators
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/ndjson/7"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "application/x-ndjson" }

  - name: gzip is decoded transparently
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/encoding/gzip"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.encoding: { equals: "gzip" }

  - name: identity needs no decoding
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/encoding/identity"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        $.encoding: { equals: "identity" }

  - name: non-utf8 body arrives intact
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/charset/iso-8859-1"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "text/plain; charset=iso-8859-1" }

  - name: binary body
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/bytes/256?seed=42"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      headers:
        Content-Type: { equals: "application/octet-stream" }
        Content-Length: { equals: "256" }

  - name: timing assertion against a known lower bound
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/echo/delay/50"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      timing:
        max_duration_ms: 5000

  - name: echo with an explicit status
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/echo/status/201"
    assertions:
      status: 201
      body:
        $.server.endpoint: { equals: "/echo/status/{code}" }

  - name: a body path can be asserted absent two ways
    request:
      method: GET
      url: "{{mud}}/json/empty"
    assertions:
      status: 200
      body:
        # not_exists and exists:false are two spellings of one intent, and they
        # disagreed until exists:false was honoured on the body path too.
        $.nothing_here: { not_exists: true }
        $.also_missing: { exists: false }
```
